{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-275576",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "This is a two-step random process: first, a room of difficulty level $n$ is chosen randomly and uniformly from $1$ to $120,$ and then a puzzle piece with a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the puzzle piece selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the room difficulty level, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $120.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the puzzle piece number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,120\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{120+1}{2}=60.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (60.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 61.5 \\\\  & =30.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
